[Music]  hello and welcome to how the light gets  in i'm jesse norman and it's my huge  honor and pleasure to be talking now to  esther duflo  whom many of you will know  as  a great economist a great champion of  the relief of poverty around the world  and a  co-winner of the nobel prize  in 2019 so on behalf of the festival on  behalf of all of our viewers esther  thank you so much for joining me and uh  what a pleasure it is to be able to talk  to you thank you so much for having me  and i'm looking forward to this um so so  esther you all appreciate that i'm i'm  going to be talking um not as someone  involved in current politics but as  someone with a background and an  interest in political economy and uh and  and specifically in the work that you do  and  one of the things that i think is so  striking  is the extent to which your work  is  uh an attempt to think  in um  in ways about economics that perhaps  aren't captured in the traditional micro  macro debates and specifically in your  pioneering use of randomized control  trials and i just wonder if we could  start with that just to bring the  audience into your work a little bit and  you could tell us a little bit about how  these trials work give some examples of  as it were great results where things  haven't gone so well and and the like  yes i'll be delighted too so  a lot of our work  is  trying to  start by unpacking the problem of  poverty  as it were which is  you could start by trying to ask a very  bold question which is you know uh does  growth help alleviate poverty or whether  foreign aid works or it does or it hurts  or or somewhere in between  and what you realize when you ask this  type of questions that they are very  difficult to answer because you know how  would you know  uh every country has a different  experience and the growth for example  might come from the tub or it might come  from the bottom with very different uh  impact on the life of the poor and of  course we don't even know what causes  growth in the first place  similarly with aid you know if you look  at africa there was more and more aid uh  over the years and more and more poverty  and the poverty impact were very limited  you might think from just looking at the  gap but of course so many things happen  so maybe it would have been much better  uh without the aid maybe it would have  been much worse without the aid we just  have no idea there is only one world  so we kind of the departure point is  that look that's not these big questions  they're you're not going to get good  answers to them so let's do something  different  potentially maybe  prima facie less ambitious but in fact  more useful  which is to say let's unpack the problem  let's say for example  education is important right everybody  agrees education is important but then  what next like what are you going to  tell for example a developing country  government about the best way to  organize their education system  then it becomes more precise  do you need more teachers  do you need a different curriculum do  you need computers do you need textbooks  so certainly you find yourself with like  thousands of  possible suggestions for how to improve  learning in schools  and there you know we can have  intuitions  but often this is intuitions are not  that good in the sense that often  when things are tried in practice they  don't work exactly the way you planned  so our idea is like look let's treat any  of these suggestions once we have  precise suggestions we can think of them  in the very same way that you think  about a vaccine for covid  which is all we have this great idea  about uh uh um you know using uh a viral  vector or using uh irm messaging well  before we launch it we try it out  so we take a sample take the covet  vaccine trials  you take a sample of about 50 000 people  you give the your candidate vaccine to  half of them randomly selected and to  the other half you don't and then  you wait over time and you see whether  they get infected with covid whether  they get very sick when they get it  hospitalized and whether they die  and  because nothing is different in the two  groups except that some got the vaccine  and some didn't  at the end of the day you can really say  with a lot of confidence that the  vaccine was effective  and it is those trials for the vaccine  that gave us like the huge gift of being  able to combat the epidemic at least in  the rich countries now why not apply the  same type of idea to social programs so  you take the education and say well my  idea is that we need to  abandon a curriculum that is too rigid  and we need to meet every child at their  current level of instruction it's not my  idea it's the idea of an organization  that we met many years ago called  pratham that was my first randomized  control trial we said great let's do  that  so in  we went in two cities bombay and baroda  took a few hundred schools  and in half of those schools they  implemented their program which was  basically a taylor education remedial  education program  for the grade three students  and in half of the school they  implemented the same program for the  grade four students  so now we can compare the great three  students where the program is in place  in grade 3 to the grade 3 student where  the program is in place in grade four  and if we find  the students who benefited from the  program to do better then we know that  it's because the program because nothing  else was different and vice versa was  great for  so we did that and we found  in this particular instance we found the  program to be effective we found that  after one year the kids who benefited  from the program through this chance did  much better  so we could replicate this experiment in  different contexts different places we  figured out that in fact that result was  a very solid  and thanks to that  pratham was able to  scale it up  they are now working with millions and  millions of children in india and today  they  this program has now moved from india to  africa  where it's being implemented again  within in about 20 countries with  various governments reaching millions of  children so that's an example of uh you  know going from the proof of concept to  the evaluation to the scale up  of of a particular intervention  that's great and of course one of the i  mean your book is full of really  interesting examples uh your first book  of cases in particular where this  approach has been taken um mosquito nets  anti-malarials nutrition um it's very  very striking the range of different  contexts um presumably there must be  circumstances in which it doesn't work i  mean in a scientific spirit you get a  new result or even possibly  counterproductive  yes that's why i could tell you with a  lot of confidence that often our  intuitions are not as good as we thought  because having been involved in many  many randomized control trials and being  part of a movement which has been  involved in many  in our lab jpal we now have about 500  researchers that are  affiliated with our movements  and uh  together they have completed you know  over a thousand studies and of this  thousand studies  i would say  at least half  give disappointed disappointing results  uh  that's not a scientific number i haven't  actually calculated but you know more  often than not things don't work as well  as you hoped  uh and some but  the point is that  that's also informative because if you  find that something doesn't work the way  you hope  then  that is giving you guns for example to  not scale it up  or to scale it down  or to  maybe reorient it i'll give you one very  prominent example  which is several years ago we started  evaluating microcredit  which at the time was uh one of the most  successful social innovation in terms of  the number of people it has reached this  is the green bank  about that everyone was talking about  that and of course professor yunus got  the the nobel prize nobel peace prize  for basically the promoting microfinance  and and disseminating it  but there had not been an evaluation of  its impact  so we didn't know there was a lot of  hope that it would be extremely  effective at uh alleviating poverty it  was very successful in terms of being  popular and reaching a lot of people but  there was no evaluation  and then  about a half dozen evaluations around  the world started springing up from  different research projects  and what was really striking is that in  none of this evaluation  the researchers found a sig any  difference really between the treatment  group that was exposed to microfinance  and the control group so that was a huge  surprise to the researchers and i think  if you had only one study you would not  believe it you would say well maybe the  study was wrong or something but when  you have half a dozen  together that really changed the  conversation  and at the beginning the microfinance  organization were of course very  disappointing bit disappointed and they  resisted a little bit the news  but then what happened is that that also  prompted a rethinking of  how they should think about their  mission and how it should organize their  mission so it becomes projective in  since we are not saying that  the fundamental problem that  microfinance is trying to address which  is  the poor don't have access to credit is  still there  uh the the question is how do we design  a solution that is actually effective  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit iai dot tv  for more debates and talks from the  world's leading thinkers on today's  biggest ideas

